1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:01,860
April fifteenth.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:04,420
Meera deposits fifteen thousand rupees at

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:05,100
the bank.

4
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:06,460
Her phone buzzes.

5
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:09,060
Your account is credited with fifteen thousand

6
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:09,860
rupees.

7
00:00:10,706 --> 00:00:11,706
Credited!

8
00:00:11,906 --> 00:00:15,266
Credit means money in, and money in is good.

9
00:00:15,426 --> 00:00:16,066
Right?

10
00:00:17,113 --> 00:00:18,513
Khata has news.

11
00:00:18,713 --> 00:00:21,593
In Meera's own books, that same deposit

12
00:00:21,673 --> 00:00:22,713
is a debit.

13
00:00:24,116 --> 00:00:25,636
So who's right?

14
00:00:31,578 --> 00:00:33,778
First, last time's questions.

15
00:00:34,058 --> 00:00:34,778
One.

16
00:00:34,858 --> 00:00:37,178
The four thousand rupee catering advance.

17
00:00:37,258 --> 00:00:38,778
Is it April's revenue?

18
00:00:39,936 --> 00:00:40,536
No.

19
00:00:40,736 --> 00:00:43,456
The stall still owes that customer an event.

20
00:00:43,496 --> 00:00:44,656
It's a liability.

21
00:00:45,211 --> 00:00:45,771
Two.

22
00:00:46,011 --> 00:00:48,611
Paying Gopal Dairy five thousand rupees.

23
00:00:48,811 --> 00:00:50,171
Is that an expense?

24
00:00:51,322 --> 00:00:51,722
No.

25
00:00:52,122 --> 00:00:54,522
Paying Gopal just shrinks what the stall

26
00:00:54,602 --> 00:00:55,322
owes him.

27
00:00:55,893 --> 00:00:56,573
Three.

28
00:00:56,853 --> 00:00:59,653
After Infotech pays four thousand, how much

29
00:00:59,733 --> 00:01:00,933
do they still owe?

30
00:01:02,082 --> 00:01:03,282
Two thousand.

31
00:01:03,362 --> 00:01:06,242
Six thousand billed, four thousand paid.

32
00:01:07,010 --> 00:01:08,610
And Aman's samosa challenge?

33
00:01:08,690 --> 00:01:11,570
Only the cash sale and the rent moved both

34
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:12,330
needles.

35
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:15,250
Aman's profit so far: eight thousand five

36
00:01:15,330 --> 00:01:16,450
hundred rupees.

37
00:01:17,714 --> 00:01:19,634
Now, that SMS.

38
00:01:20,114 --> 00:01:22,354
Debit and credit come from Latin.

39
00:01:22,594 --> 00:01:24,154
Debere, to owe.

40
00:01:24,354 --> 00:01:26,114
Credere, to trust.

41
00:01:26,753 --> 00:01:28,193
But here's the secret.

42
00:01:28,433 --> 00:01:29,633
Forget those meanings.

43
00:01:30,033 --> 00:01:32,833
In accounting, debit just means left.

44
00:01:33,593 --> 00:01:35,593
And credit just means right.

45
00:01:36,514 --> 00:01:39,154
Remember Khata's two pages, from the very

46
00:01:39,234 --> 00:01:40,114
first lesson?

47
00:01:40,354 --> 00:01:41,314
This is why.

48
00:01:42,365 --> 00:01:44,045
So which side do you write on?

49
00:01:44,525 --> 00:01:45,565
Look at the scale.

50
00:01:45,805 --> 00:01:47,125
Assets on the left.

51
00:01:47,325 --> 00:01:49,485
Liabilities and equity on the right.

52
00:01:49,885 --> 00:01:52,445
Every account has a home side: the side

53
00:01:52,525 --> 00:01:53,485
where it lives.

54
00:01:54,041 --> 00:01:55,401
And here's the whole rule.

55
00:01:55,881 --> 00:01:58,521
When an account grows, write it on its home

56
00:01:58,601 --> 00:01:59,121
side.

57
00:01:59,921 --> 00:02:02,481
When it shrinks, write it on the other side.

58
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:04,810
April first.

59
00:02:04,890 --> 00:02:07,370
Meera puts in fifty thousand rupees.

60
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:08,970
Which two things changed?

61
00:02:10,937 --> 00:02:11,737
Cash went up.

62
00:02:11,817 --> 00:02:14,217
An asset, home on the left.

63
00:02:14,617 --> 00:02:15,817
Debit Cash.

64
00:02:16,557 --> 00:02:17,677
Capital went up.

65
00:02:17,917 --> 00:02:19,757
Equity, home on the right.

66
00:02:19,917 --> 00:02:21,077
Credit Capital.

67
00:02:21,277 --> 00:02:23,917
Fifty thousand left, fifty thousand right.

68
00:02:24,671 --> 00:02:27,711
Now the cart, thirty-six thousand rupees.

69
00:02:27,951 --> 00:02:29,551
Which two things changed?

70
00:02:31,504 --> 00:02:32,904
Equipment grows.

71
00:02:33,104 --> 00:02:35,304
An asset, home on the left.

72
00:02:35,504 --> 00:02:36,784
Debit Equipment.

73
00:02:37,684 --> 00:02:39,324
But Cash shrinks.

74
00:02:39,524 --> 00:02:42,164
Cash lives on the left, so to shrink it,

75
00:02:42,244 --> 00:02:43,244
write on the right.

76
00:02:43,444 --> 00:02:44,804
Credit Cash.

77
00:02:46,352 --> 00:02:48,912
So debit doesn't always mean up.

78
00:02:49,932 --> 00:02:51,292
It depends on the home.

79
00:02:52,558 --> 00:02:54,798
Liabilities work the same way, from the

80
00:02:54,878 --> 00:02:55,758
other side.

81
00:02:55,838 --> 00:02:57,838
Gopal Dairy sends eight thousand rupees

82
00:02:57,918 --> 00:02:59,598
of stock, pay later.

83
00:02:59,678 --> 00:03:00,878
Stock grows.

84
00:03:00,958 --> 00:03:02,158
Debit Stock.

85
00:03:02,238 --> 00:03:03,198
And Gopal?

86
00:03:03,278 --> 00:03:04,718
Debit, or credit?

87
00:03:06,278 --> 00:03:06,998
Credit.

88
00:03:07,238 --> 00:03:10,598
What the stall owes Gopal grew, and liabilities

89
00:03:10,758 --> 00:03:11,718
live on the right.

90
00:03:12,786 --> 00:03:13,906
April fifth.

91
00:03:14,146 --> 00:03:16,146
Rent, five thousand rupees.

92
00:03:16,386 --> 00:03:17,626
Cash shrinks.

93
00:03:17,826 --> 00:03:18,826
Credit Cash.

94
00:03:19,526 --> 00:03:20,286
And Rent?

95
00:03:20,646 --> 00:03:21,846
Rent grew.

96
00:03:21,926 --> 00:03:23,526
But where is its home?

97
00:03:24,273 --> 00:03:25,953
Remember the long equation?

98
00:03:26,193 --> 00:03:28,993
Assets equal liabilities, plus capital,

99
00:03:29,113 --> 00:03:31,553
plus revenue, minus expenses.

100
00:03:32,273 --> 00:03:34,153
That minus is the clue.

101
00:03:34,233 --> 00:03:36,713
Move Expenses across the equals sign, and

102
00:03:36,753 --> 00:03:38,553
the minus becomes a plus.

103
00:03:39,109 --> 00:03:42,389
Assets, plus expenses, equal liabilities,

104
00:03:42,509 --> 00:03:44,629
plus capital, plus revenue.

105
00:03:46,209 --> 00:03:48,929
So expenses live on the left, with the assets.

106
00:03:49,169 --> 00:03:51,809
Rent grew, and the left is its home.

107
00:03:51,969 --> 00:03:53,249
Debit Rent.

108
00:03:53,909 --> 00:03:55,589
Every expense is a debit.

109
00:03:56,355 --> 00:03:59,155
Revenue never moved, so its home stays on

110
00:03:59,235 --> 00:04:00,835
the right, with capital.

111
00:04:01,075 --> 00:04:03,075
Every sale is a credit.

112
00:04:04,142 --> 00:04:04,942
Put it together.

113
00:04:05,422 --> 00:04:08,622
Debits live on the left: Expenses, Assets,

114
00:04:08,662 --> 00:04:11,262
and Drawings, the money Meera takes home.

115
00:04:11,342 --> 00:04:13,022
They behave like expenses.

116
00:04:13,572 --> 00:04:16,252
Credits live on the right: Liabilities,

117
00:04:16,452 --> 00:04:19,332
Income, that's revenue, or income, same

118
00:04:19,452 --> 00:04:20,772
thing, and Capital.

119
00:04:21,252 --> 00:04:22,772
In short: DEAD CLIC.

120
00:04:23,252 --> 00:04:24,532
You didn't memorise this.

121
00:04:24,692 --> 00:04:26,772
You read it straight off the equation.

122
00:04:27,338 --> 00:04:30,138
And in every transaction, the left total

123
00:04:30,378 --> 00:04:31,818
equals the right total.

124
00:04:32,138 --> 00:04:32,858
Always.

125
00:04:33,578 --> 00:04:36,018
That's double entry: every rupee written

126
00:04:36,098 --> 00:04:38,418
twice, once on each side.

127
00:04:38,498 --> 00:04:40,818
The same scale, in a new costume.

128
00:04:42,064 --> 00:04:44,064
So, the SMS.

129
00:04:44,624 --> 00:04:46,864
Two cameras, one deposit.

130
00:04:46,944 --> 00:04:49,504
In Meera's books, money moved from the galla

131
00:04:49,584 --> 00:04:50,544
to the bank.

132
00:04:50,624 --> 00:04:51,824
Bank grows.

133
00:04:51,864 --> 00:04:52,864
Debit Bank.

134
00:04:53,064 --> 00:04:54,064
Cash shrinks.

135
00:04:54,224 --> 00:04:55,344
Credit Cash.

136
00:04:55,908 --> 00:04:58,228
In the bank's books, the notes land in its

137
00:04:58,308 --> 00:04:59,028
vault.

138
00:04:59,108 --> 00:05:00,468
That's its debit.

139
00:05:00,548 --> 00:05:02,948
But Meera's fifteen thousand isn't the bank's

140
00:05:03,028 --> 00:05:03,508
money.

141
00:05:03,748 --> 00:05:06,148
It's money the bank owes her, any day she

142
00:05:06,228 --> 00:05:06,828
asks.

143
00:05:07,028 --> 00:05:09,308
For the bank, that's a liability.

144
00:05:09,508 --> 00:05:12,068
It grows, so the bank credits it.

145
00:05:12,625 --> 00:05:13,345
Same money.

146
00:05:13,425 --> 00:05:14,945
Opposite books.

147
00:05:15,785 --> 00:05:18,585
Your SMS is reading the bank's khata, not

148
00:05:18,665 --> 00:05:19,305
yours.

149
00:05:19,625 --> 00:05:21,385
Credit doesn't mean good.

150
00:05:21,465 --> 00:05:23,385
Debit doesn't mean bad.

151
00:05:23,465 --> 00:05:25,625
They just mean right, and left.

152
00:05:26,881 --> 00:05:27,881
Let's practise.

153
00:05:28,081 --> 00:05:29,241
Khata goes first.

154
00:05:29,521 --> 00:05:31,841
Cash sales for the first half of April:

155
00:05:31,921 --> 00:05:33,401
eighteen thousand rupees.

156
00:05:33,681 --> 00:05:35,281
Which two things changed?

157
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:37,960
Cash grew.

158
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:40,760
Debit Cash, eighteen thousand.

159
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:41,880
Sales grew.

160
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:44,520
That's revenue, so credit Sales, eighteen

161
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:45,480
thousand.

162
00:05:45,560 --> 00:05:47,080
Left equals right.

163
00:05:47,836 --> 00:05:48,636
Meera's turn.

164
00:05:49,116 --> 00:05:51,916
Infotech's chai tab: six thousand rupees,

165
00:05:52,156 --> 00:05:52,876
pay later.

166
00:05:53,116 --> 00:05:55,836
The chai was sold, so credit Sales, six

167
00:05:55,996 --> 00:05:56,876
thousand.

168
00:05:56,956 --> 00:05:57,996
Now Infotech.

169
00:05:58,396 --> 00:05:59,676
They owe the stall.

170
00:05:59,916 --> 00:06:01,836
Meera slides it toward the right page.

171
00:06:02,236 --> 00:06:04,836
Owing money, that was Gopal's side.

172
00:06:05,036 --> 00:06:05,756
Wasn't it?

173
00:06:08,104 --> 00:06:08,504
No.

174
00:06:08,904 --> 00:06:10,744
Gopal is owed by the stall.

175
00:06:11,224 --> 00:06:13,024
Infotech owes the stall.

176
00:06:13,224 --> 00:06:14,904
An asset, and it grew.

177
00:06:15,304 --> 00:06:16,744
Home side, left.

178
00:06:16,984 --> 00:06:19,144
Debit Infotech, six thousand.

179
00:06:20,062 --> 00:06:21,382
Now, just you.

180
00:06:21,662 --> 00:06:24,622
Meera pays Gopal Dairy five thousand rupees.

181
00:06:24,862 --> 00:06:25,622
Debit what?

182
00:06:25,822 --> 00:06:26,702
Credit what?

183
00:06:29,867 --> 00:06:31,307
Debit Gopal Dairy.

184
00:06:31,787 --> 00:06:34,187
The liability shrinks, so it goes on the

185
00:06:34,387 --> 00:06:34,827
left.

186
00:06:35,067 --> 00:06:36,507
Credit Cash.

187
00:06:36,587 --> 00:06:38,427
Five thousand leaves the galla.

188
00:06:39,683 --> 00:06:40,403
So.

189
00:06:40,483 --> 00:06:42,963
Debit is left, credit is right.

190
00:06:43,083 --> 00:06:45,203
Grow an account on its home side, shrink

191
00:06:45,283 --> 00:06:46,403
it on the other.

192
00:06:46,443 --> 00:06:49,043
And the bank's SMS speaks from the bank's

193
00:06:49,283 --> 00:06:49,843
books.

194
00:06:51,053 --> 00:06:51,693
Your turn.

195
00:06:51,853 --> 00:06:52,853
Three quick ones.

196
00:06:53,053 --> 00:06:53,533
One.

197
00:06:53,773 --> 00:06:56,493
Meera buys six thousand rupees of tea, milk

198
00:06:56,573 --> 00:06:58,373
and sugar, and pays cash.

199
00:06:58,573 --> 00:07:00,573
What's debited, and what's credited?

200
00:07:01,133 --> 00:07:01,613
Two.

201
00:07:01,853 --> 00:07:03,933
Why is an expense a debit?

202
00:07:04,253 --> 00:07:04,813
Three.

203
00:07:05,053 --> 00:07:07,133
Imagine Meera pays a bill from the bank,

204
00:07:07,213 --> 00:07:09,053
and the SMS says debited.

205
00:07:09,453 --> 00:07:12,413
In Meera's books, is Bank debited, or credited?

206
00:07:12,893 --> 00:07:14,573
Answers next lesson.

207
00:07:15,993 --> 00:07:18,553
Next time, Meera's CA friend drops by the

208
00:07:18,633 --> 00:07:20,393
stall, with rules.

209
00:07:20,633 --> 00:07:21,673
Debit the receiver!

210
00:07:21,833 --> 00:07:22,913
Credit the giver!

211
00:07:23,113 --> 00:07:25,113
Is that a whole new system to learn?
